contentText:
AI music company Suno, led by CEO Mikey Shulman , posted a set of principles on its website Thursday (Aug. 6). “We&#8217;ve always believed that how we build matters just as much as what we build,” wrote Shulman in a company blog post. The list of principles included the stated goals that “AI should enable originality, not imitation” and that “more people creating music should strengthen the ecosystem.” The post also reiterated the company’s plan to limit downloads of AI songs from its platform — something first promised during its settlement and licensing deal with Warner Music Group.

The new post comes just a week after the AI music giant, recently valued at over $5.4 billion , lost a copyright infringement battle with German collection society GEMA. In the lawsuit, the society accused Suno of replicating songs from their members, including Lou Bega’s classic “Mambo No. 5.” A source notes that Suno&#8217;s announcement on Thursday was in motion prior to the ruling.

