“AI should help people create something new, not imitate someone else’s work,” the Suno post reads. “This philosophy has guided how we’ve built our models and platform from the start.” It continued to detail Suno’s training strategies, dubbed “Original Creation, By Design,” to reduce the possibility of unauthorized recreation of a human-made song.&nbsp;“For example, we intentionally chose not to use artist names in our training metadata because our goal is to help people create original songs — not music that sounds like existing artists,&#8221; he wrote.

Suno’s platform also prevents users typing in specific artist names to prompt results in their style. This, however, has not prevented all instances of unauthorized replication of copyrights included in its dataset. To date, the company has been hit with several copyright infringement lawsuits — including those filed by GEMA, Danish rights organization Koda and the three major music companies: Sony Music, Universal Music Group and Warner Music Group. (WMG has since settled their part of the lawsuit with Suno and struck a licensing deal. UMG and Sony continue to pursue the case.)

The company blog post also pointed out that download limits for Suno-generated songs are coming soon. This plan was first announced in late 2025 and was a stipulation of Suno’s settlement with WMG. The new post does not state exactly when this will be rolled out, but it teased that “these changes won’t affect the vast majority of our users, but they will make large-scale abuse much harder,” wrote Shulman.

He added that “while we don’t think it’s our role to decide what has artistic value, we have an important responsibility to make it harder to abuse AI tools in ways that undermine a healthy music ecosystem,” like the mass exporting of AI songs, which has become a popular method for bad actors to spread out artificial streaming schemes over a large number of tracks and to better evade detection.

AI songs making their way onto streaming services, charts and social media has long been a concern for the music industry establishment. The majors noted this in their lawsuit against Suno, which began in June 2024, saying AI music “saturate[s] the market” and “directly compete[s] with cheapen[s] and ultimately drown[s] out the genuine sound recordings.&#8221;

Since then, the majors’ opinions on what should be done to mitigate the flood of AI music have somewhat diverged. Universal feels strongly about AI music companies being what its chief digital officer and executive vp Michael Nash calls “walled gardens,” meaning the platforms become closed sandboxes with no downloading allowed. In an interview with Billboard , Nash was asked if Suno&#8217;s lack of &#8220;walled garden&#8221; was the reason why UMG had not been able to reach a settlement with it yet. Nash replied: &#8220;If I treated your question as a rhetorical question, I would say yes, having articulated the walled garden distinction and its importance [for UMG,] that’s kind of a hat-hanger in this discussion.&#8221; Warner, on the other hand, agreed to settle with Suno, satisfied by a promised limit on downloads. Sony’s stance on the matter is unclear.

In July, a record industry coalition released a proposal for how streaming services could treat the impact of AI-generated tracks on their platforms. This included the suggestion of adding labels to any AI-generated or AI-assisted recording. Disclosure of AI use would be voluntary by the user, and while the proposal gained widespread support among the labels, it is still unclear if any streaming service will agree to implement their ideas.

